To solve this problem, we need to implement a generic merge sort algorithm that handles both ascending and descending order based on a provided comparator and a boolean flag. The solution must include a stable merge method that preserves the relative order of equal elements during the merging process.

### Approach
1. **Generic Implementation**: The solution uses generics to support sorting any type of array, provided a comparator.
2. **Stable Merge**: The merge logic ensures stability by always preferring elements from the left array when elements are equal, regardless of the sorting direction.
3. **Recursive Divide and Conquer**: The array is recursively divided into halves until base cases (single element arrays) are reached, then merged back in sorted order.
4. **Order Handling**: The merge logic uses the ascending flag to determine the comparison direction, ensuring correct sorting order.

### Solution Code
```java
import java.util.Comparator;

class EnhancedMergeSort {

    public Integer[] sortIntegers(Integer[] array) {
        return sort(array, Comparator.naturalOrder(), true);
    }

    public <T> T[] sort(T[] array, Comparator<? super T> comparator, boolean ascending) {
        if (array == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Array must not be null");
        }
        if (comparator == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Comparator must not be null");
        }
        if (array.length <= 1) {
            return array;
        }
        return mergeSort(array, 0, array.length - 1, comparator, ascending);
    }

    private <T> T[] mergeSort(T[] array, int left, int right, Comparator<? super T> comparator, boolean ascending) {
        if (left < right) {
            int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;
            mergeSort(array, left, mid, comparator, ascending);
            mergeSort(array, mid + 1, right, comparator, ascending);
            merge(array, left, mid, right, comparator, ascending);
        }
        return array;
    }

    private <T> void merge(T[] array, int left, int mid, int right, Comparator<? super T> comparator, boolean ascending) {
        int leftLength = mid - left + 1;
        int rightLength = right - mid;

        @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
        T[] leftArray = (T[]) new Object[leftLength];
        @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
        T[] rightArray = (T[]) new Object[rightLength];

        for (int i = 0; i < leftLength; i++) {
            leftArray[i] = array[left + i];
        }
        for (int j = 0; j < rightLength; j++) {
            rightArray[j] = array[mid + 1 + j];
        }

        @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
        T[] targetArray = (T[]) new Object[leftLength + rightLength];
        mergeArrays(leftArray, rightArray, targetArray, comparator, ascending);

        System.arraycopy(targetArray, 0, array, left, targetArray.length);
    }

    private static <T> void mergeArrays(T[] leftArray, T[] rightArray, T[] targetArray, 
                                       Comparator<? super T> comparator, boolean ascending) {
        int i = 0, j = 0, k = 0;

        while (i < leftArray.length && j < rightArray.length) {
            int comparison = comparator.compare(leftArray[i], rightArray[j]);
            boolean condition = ascending ? comparison <= 0 : comparison >= 0;

            if (condition) {
                targetArray[k++] = leftArray[i++];
            } else {
                targetArray[k++] = rightArray[j++];
            }
        }

        while (i < leftArray.length) {
            targetArray[k++] = leftArray[i++];
        }

        while (j < rightArray.length) {
            targetArray[k++] = rightArray[j++];
        }
    }
}
